<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Inançids</h1><p class="st-meta">1261 CE – 1368 CE (approx.) · capital Denizli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Turkmen beylik around Denizli (Ladik) in south-western Anatolia, visited by Ibn Battuta.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the later 13th century, as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> weakened, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> frontier warriors took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denizli">Ladik</a>, near ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laodicea-on-the-lycus">Laodicea</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycus-phrygia">Lycus</a> valley. A local dynasty took shape there under Mehmed Bey and later took its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanc-bey">Inanc Bey</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>'s government. It lay on the trade road through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Meander</a> valley and produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> textiles embroidered with gold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> admired when he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denizli">Ladik</a> around 1333, describing its ruler as generous. The beylik often depended on the stronger <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/germiyanids">Germiyanids</a> to the north.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1368 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/germiyanids">Germiyanids</a> absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denizli">Ladik</a>, and the region later passed with Germiyan lands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1261 CE – 1368 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.80%;width:2.13%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1261 CE – 1368 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Denizli</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mehmed Bey, Inanc Bey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ladik beylik, İnançoğulları</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1314&amp;focus=inancids" aria-label="Inançids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5406 819 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Inançids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5726 1075L5741 1021"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5741 1021h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5726 1075h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5726 1075h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Denizli. <a href="/atlas?year=1314&amp;focus=inancids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

